# Lecture 07C

## Probability of progeny, part 2

## Chapter 4

## Dr. Neil Voss

## October 20, 2026

=== layout: one-panel

# Learning objectives

@body

- Condition genotype probabilities on survival.
- Explain why two offspring distributions can be equally likely.
- Extend binomial reasoning to three or four categories.

=== layout: section

# Challenge Problem #2

=== layout: one-panel

# Challenge: five surviving mice

@body

- Tt: short tail; tt: normal long tail
- TT: lethal before the surviving litter is counted
- Cross: Tt &times; Tt

*What is the distribution of short-tailed and long-tailed mice in five survivors?*

Why are the two most likely outcomes equally probable?

@notes

The legacy problem uses short-tailed and tailless interchangeably. Use short-tailed consistently.

=== layout: one-panel

# Four equally likely zygotes

@body

| Gametes | T | t |
| --- | --- | --- |
| T | TT | Tt |
| t | Tt | tt |

=== layout: one-panel

# Remove the lethal TT genotype

@body

| Gametes | T | t |
| --- | --- | --- |
| T | TT (lethal) | Tt |
| t | Tt | tt |

@notes

Among survivors, two of the three equally likely surviving genotype outcomes are Tt.

=== layout: one-panel

# Condition on survival

@body

| Surviving genotype | Relative count | Probability |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tt: short tail | 2 | 2/3 |
| tt: long tail | 1 | 1/3 |

@notes

Exclude TT before defining the probabilities for a litter of survivors.

=== layout: one-panel

# Count short-tailed survivors

@body

- Among survivors: P(Tt) = 2/3; P(tt) = 1/3
- k = number of short-tailed mice among five survivors

*P(k) = C(5,k) &times; (2/3)^k^ &times; (1/3)^(5-k)^*

= C(5,k) &times; 2^k^ / 3^5^

=== layout: one-panel

# Five survivors: all six outcomes

@body

| Short tail | Long tail | Probability | Percent |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 5 | 1/243 | 0.4% |
| 1 | 4 | 10/243 | 4.1% |
| 2 | 3 | 40/243 | 16.5% |
| 3 | 2 | 80/243 | 32.9% |
| 4 | 1 | 80/243 | 32.9% |
| 5 | 0 | 32/243 | 13.2% |

=== layout: one-panel

# One formula for all outcomes

@body

*P(k short tails) = C(5,k) &times; 2^k^ / 243*

- C(5,k) counts orders.
- 2^k^ weights short-tailed outcomes.
- The shared denominator is 3^5^ = 243.

Which k values give the largest probability?

=== layout: one-panel

# Why two modes?

@body

- Three short tails: C(5,3) &times; 2^3^ / 243 = 80/243
- Four short tails: C(5,4) &times; 2^4^ / 243 = 80/243

*Both are about 32.9%.*

More orders for k = 3 balance the greater weight per order for k = 4.

=== layout: section

# Equally Likely Combinations

=== layout: one-panel

# Four boys among seven children

@body

- C(7,4) = 35 possible birth orders
- Each order has probability (1/2)^7^.

*P(4 boys and 3 girls) = 35/128 &asymp; 27.3%*

=== layout: one-panel

# Either four boys or four girls

@body

- P(4 boys and 3 girls) = 35/128
- P(3 boys and 4 girls) = 35/128
- Mutually exclusive outcomes: add.

*P(either) = 70/128 = 35/64 &asymp; 54.7%*

=== layout: one-panel

# Six children: probability review

@body

| Girls | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Orders | 1 | 6 | 15 | 20 | 15 | 6 | 1 |
| Probability | 1/64 | 6/64 | 15/64 | 20/64 | 15/64 | 6/64 | 1/64 |

@notes

At least three boys means at most three girls: (1 + 6 + 15 + 20)/64.

