=== layout: one-panel

# Three-category problem

@body

Seven independent offspring; three equally likely categories: &alpha;, &beta;, &gamma;.

*Count exactly 3 &alpha;, 2 &beta;, and 2 &gamma;.*

- Choose positions for &alpha;: C(7,3) = 35
- Choose &beta; from the four remaining positions: C(4,2) = 6
- The remaining two positions are &gamma;: C(2,2) = 1

*Orders = 35 &times; 6 &times; 1 = 210*

=== layout: one-panel

# Three-category problem

@body

Seven independent offspring; three equally likely categories: &alpha;, &beta;, &gamma;.

*Count exactly 3 &alpha;, 2 &beta;, and 2 &gamma;.*

- Choose positions for &alpha;: C(7,3) = 35
- Choose &beta; from the four remaining positions: C(4,2) = 6
- The remaining two positions are &gamma;: C(2,2) = 1

*Orders = 35 &times; 6 &times; 1 = 210*

P = 210 &times; (1/3)^7^ = 210/2187 &asymp; 9.6%

=== layout: one-panel

# Three-category problem

@body

Seven independent offspring; three equally likely categories: &alpha;, &beta;, &gamma;.

*Count exactly 3 &alpha;, 2 &beta;, and 2 &gamma;.*

- Choose positions for &alpha;: C(7,3) = 35
- Choose &beta; from the four remaining positions: C(4,2) = 6
- The remaining two positions are &gamma;: C(2,2) = 1

*Orders = 35 &times; 6 &times; 1 = 210*

P = 210 &times; (1/3)^7^ = 210/2187 &asymp; 9.6%

=== layout: one-panel

# Three-category problem

@body

- Choose &alpha; first: C(7,3) C(4,2) C(2,2) = 210
- Choose &beta; first: C(7,2) C(5,3) C(2,2) = 210
- Choose &beta;, then &gamma;: C(7,2) C(5,2) C(3,3) = 210

*The order of choosing categories does not change the number of arrangements.*

