=== layout: section

# Exact Dihybrid Cross

=== layout: one-panel

# *Dihybrid Cross*

@body

- A typical dihybrid cross produces a phenotype ratio of 9\:3\:3\:1.
- [What is the probability of obtaining exactly 9, 3, 3, and 1 from only 16 offspring in a dihybrid cross?]{color=red}

=== layout: one-panel

# Four categories, not two

@body

- AaBb &times; AaBb; independently assorting loci
- Expected phenotype probabilities: 9/16, 3/16, 3/16, 1/16
- Target counts: 9 A-B-, 3 A-bb, 3 aaB-, 1 aabb

*How many arrangements give those counts in 16 offspring?*

=== layout: one-panel

# Expand the choose functions

@body

- Label the four categories YR, Yw, gR, gw.
- Counts: a, b, c, d; total n = a+b+c+d

*Arrangements = n! / (a! b! c! d!)*

*P = arrangements &times; p~YR~^a^ p~Yw~^b^ p~gR~^c^ p~gw~^d^*

Same logic: count orders, then multiply by the probability of one order.

=== layout: one-panel

# Exactly 9:3:3:1 among 16 offspring

@body

- Arrangements: C(16,9) C(7,3) C(4,3) C(1,1)
- = 11,440 &times; 35 &times; 4 &times; 1 = 1,601,600

*P = 1,601,600 &times; (9/16)^9^ &times; (3/16)^6^ &times; (1/16)*

*&asymp; 2.45%*

An expected ratio is not a promise of exact counts.

=== layout: section

# Summary

=== layout: one-panel

# Pascal's triangle: n = 0 through 5

@body

| n | Coefficients from k = 0 through n |
| --- | --- |
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 1  1 |
| 2 | 1  2  1 |
| 3 | 1  3  3  1 |
| 4 | 1  4  6  4  1 |
| 5 | 1  5  10  10  5  1 |

@notes

Read across row n. The first entry is k = 0. Entries count arrangements, not probabilities.

=== layout: one-panel

# Pascal's triangle: n = 6 through 10

@body

| n | Coefficients from k = 0 through n |
| --- | --- |
| 6 | 1  6  15  20  15  6  1 |
| 7 | 1  7  21  35  35  21  7  1 |
| 8 | 1  8  28  56  70  56  28  8  1 |
| 9 | 1  9  36  84  126  126  84  36  9  1 |
| 10 | 1  10  45  120  210  252  210  120  45  10  1 |

@notes

Read across row n. The first entry is k = 0. Entries count arrangements, not probabilities.

